## Concept Overview
A custom useDebounce hook defers function execution until a specified delay period has elapsed, making it essential for optimizing search inputs, API calls, and other performance-critical operations in React applications.

## Implementation Details
- Utilize useRef to maintain the latest callback reference, preventing stale closures across renders
- Implement comprehensive cleanup logic in useEffect to cancel pending debounced invocations
- Return a useCallback wrapper with stable dependencies for optimal concurrent rendering performance
- Handle component unmounting scenarios to prevent memory leaks and unexpected behavior
- Ensure proper cleanup of timers to maintain application stability

## Code Example
```javascript
import { useCallback, useRef, useEffect } from 'react';

export function useDebounce(callback, delay) {
  const callbackRef = useRef(callback);
  const timeoutRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    callbackRef.current = callback;
  }, [callback]);

  useEffect(() => {
    return () => {
      if (timeoutRef.current) {
        clearTimeout(timeoutRef.current);
      }
    };
  }, []);

  return useCallback((...args) => {
    if (timeoutRef.current) {
      clearTimeout(timeoutRef.current);
    }

    timeoutRef.current = setTimeout(() => {
      callbackRef.current(...args);
    }, delay);
  }, [delay]);
}
```

## Key Benefits
- Prevents stale closures through proper ref management
- Ensures cleanup on component unmount
- Maintains stable reference for concurrent rendering
- Handles edge cases and prevents memory leaks

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to architect a simple, scalable UI state pattern with Context + useReducer, while handling real-time updates and preventing unnecessary re-renders. It also exercises memoization and debouncing—common patterns in feature-flag management UIs.

## Key Concepts
- React Context + useReducer for centralized state management
- React.memo for selective re-render optimization
- Debouncing external updates to reduce server load
- Clean-up on unmount to prevent memory leaks

## Code Example
```javascript
// FeatureFlagContext.tsx
import React, { createContext, useContext, useReducer, useEffect, useRef, useCallback } from 'react';

type Action =
  | { type: 'SET_FLAG'; name: string; value: boolean }
  | { type: 'PATCH_FLAGS'; patches: Record<string, boolean> };

type State = Record<string, boolean>;

const reducer = (state: State, action: Action): State => {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'SET_FLAG':
      return { ...state, [action.name]: action.value };
    case 'PATCH_FLAGS':
      return { ...state, ...action.patches };
    default:
      return state;
  }
};

const FeatureFlagContext = createContext<{
  flags: State;
  dispatch: React.Dispatch<Action>;
} | null>(null);

// Debounce hook
const useDebounce = (callback: () => void, delay: number) => {
  const timeoutRef = useRef<NodeJS.Timeout>();
  const callbackRef = useRef(callback);

  useEffect(() => { callbackRef.current = callback; });

  useEffect(() => {
    return () => clearTimeout(timeoutRef.current);
  }, []);

  return useCallback(() => {
    clearTimeout(timeoutRef.current);
    timeoutRef.current = setTimeout(() => callbackRef.current(), delay);
  }, [delay]);
};

export const FeatureFlagProvider: React.FC<{ children: React.ReactNode }> = ({ children }) => {
  const [flags, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, {});
  const debouncedUpdate = useDebounce(() => {
    // Push flag changes to server
    fetch('/api/flags', { method: 'PATCH', body: JSON.stringify(flags) });
  }, 200);

  useEffect(() => { debouncedUpdate(); }, [flags]);

  return (
    <FeatureFlagContext.Provider value={{ flags, dispatch }}>
      {children}
    </FeatureFlagContext.Provider>
  );
};

// Custom hook
export const useFeatureFlag = (name: string): [boolean, (value: boolean) => void] => {
  const { flags, dispatch } = useContext(FeatureFlagContext)!;
  const enabled = flags[name] ?? false;
  const setEnabled = useCallback((value: boolean) => {
    dispatch({ type: 'SET_FLAG', name, value });
  }, [name]);

  return [enabled, setEnabled];
};

// Debug Panel
const FlagRow = React.memo(({ name, value, onToggle }: { name: string; value: boolean; onToggle: () => void }) => (
  <div>
    <span>{name}</span>
    <button onClick={onToggle}>{value ? 'ON' : 'OFF'}</button>
  </div>
));

export const DebugPanel = ({ flags, dispatch }: { flags: State; dispatch: React.Dispatch<Action> }) => {
  return (
    <div>
      {Object.entries(flags).map(([name, value]) => (
        <FlagRow
          key={name}
          name={name}
          value={value}
          onToggle={() => dispatch({ type: 'SET_FLAG', name, value: !value })}
        />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced CSS Grid mastery, performance optimization skills, and ability to handle large-scale UI challenges - critical for Google's complex web applications.

## Expected Answer

Strong candidates discuss CSS Grid auto-fit/minmax, virtual scrolling implementation, CSS transform-based animations for GPU acceleration, and batched DOM updates using requestAnimationFrame or DocumentFragment.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Virtualized grid with performant updates
class VirtualizedGrid {
  constructor(container, data) {
    this.container = container;
    this.data = data;
 this.visibleItems = [];
    this.itemHeight = 50;
    this.bufferSize = 5;

    this.setupGrid();
    this.bindEvents();
  }

  setupGrid() {
    this.container.style.display = 'grid';
    this.container.style.gridTemplateColumns = 'repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr))';
    this.container.style.gap = '16px';
    this.container.style.height = '400px';
    this.container.style.overflow = 'auto';
  }

  updateVisibleItems() {
    const scrollTop = this.container.scrollTop;
    const containerHeight = this.container.clientHeight;
    const startIndex = Math.max(0, Math.floor(scrollTop / this.itemHeight) - this.bufferSize);
    const endIndex = Math.min(this.data.length, Math.ceil((scrollTop + containerHeight) / this.itemHeight) + this.bufferSize);

    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      this.renderItems(startIndex, endIndex);
    });
  }

  renderItems(startIndex, endIndex) {
    const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment();

    // Clear existing items
    this.container.innerHTML = '';

    // Render visible items
    for (let i = startIndex; i < endIndex; i++) {
      const item = this.createItem(this.data[i], i);
      fragment.appendChild(item);
    }

    this.container.appendChild(fragment);
  }

  createItem(data, index) {
    const item = document.createElement('div');
    item.className = 'grid-item';
    item.textContent = data.title;
    item.style.transform = `translateY(${index * this.itemHeight}px)`;
    item.style.transition = 'transform 0.3s ease';

    // Use transform for position changes (GPU accelerated)
    item.addEventListener('click', () => {
      item.style.transform = `translateY(${index * this.itemHeight}px) scale(1.05)`;
      setTimeout(() => {
        item.style.transform = `translateY(${index * this.itemHeight}px) scale(1)`;
      }, 200);
    });

    return item;
  }

  bindEvents() {
    this.container.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
      this.updateVisibleItems();
    });

    window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
      this.updateVisibleItems();
    });
  }

  // Batch update for large datasets
  updateData(newData) {
    this.data = newData;

    // Use requestAnimationFrame for smooth updates
    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      this.updateVisibleItems();
    });
  }

  // Reorder items with animation
  reorderItems(fromIndex, toIndex) {
    const item = this.data[fromIndex];
    this.data.splice(fromIndex, 1);
    this.data.splice(toIndex, 0, item);

    // Animate the reordering
    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      this.updateVisibleItems();
    });
  }
}

// Performance monitoring
const performanceMonitor = {
  measureFPS() {
    let lastTime = performance.now();
    let frames = 0;

    function measure() {
      frames++;
      const currentTime = performance.now();

      if (currentTime >= lastTime + 1000) {
        const fps = Math.round((frames * 1000) / (currentTime - lastTime));
        console.log(`FPS: ${fps}`);
        frames = 0;
        lastTime = currentTime;
      }

      requestAnimationFrame(measure);
    }

    requestAnimationFrame(measure);
  }
};
```

## Solution Overview
Use closure to encapsulate rate limiter state with request queue and timer-based enforcement.

## Key Components
- Queue array for pending requests
- Processing flag to prevent race conditions
- Promise chaining for FIFO ordering
- Timer-based rate limit enforcement

## Implementation
```javascript
const createRateLimiter = (limit, interval) => {
  const queue = [];
  let processing = false;
  let lastRequest = 0;

  return async (request) => {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      queue.push({ request, resolve, reject });
      processQueue();
    });
  };

  async function processQueue() {
    if (processing || queue.length === 0) return;
    processing = true;

    const now = Date.now();
    const timeSinceLastRequest = now - lastRequest;
    const delay = timeSinceLastRequest < interval ? interval - timeSinceLastRequest : 0;

    setTimeout(async () => {
      const { request, resolve, reject } = queue.shift();
      lastRequest = Date.now();

      try {
        const result = await request();
        resolve(result);
      } catch (error) {
        reject(error);
      }

      processing = false;
      processQueue();
    }, delay);
  }
};
```

## Benefits
- Maintains request order through FIFO queue
- Prevents rate limit violations with timing control
- Handles concurrent requests gracefully

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of closures, promise sharing, and robust batching logic in the UI. It probes how a candidate handles in-flight deduping, race conditions, and cache invalidation in a realistic data-fetching utility.

## Key Concepts
- Closures to encapsulate private state (inFlight, cache, batch)
- Shared Promises for identical keys to prevent duplicate fetches
- Debounced batching with batchDelay and maxBatchSize
- Cache population and correct mapping from response to keys
- Error handling and late responses without memory leaks

## Code Example
```javascript
function createBatcher(fetchFn, { batchDelay = 50, maxBatchSize = 20 } = {}) {
  const cache = new Map();
  const inFlight = new Map();
  let batch = new Set();
  let timer = null;

  function flush() {
    const keys = Array.from(batch).slice(0, maxBatchSize);
    batch.clear();
    timer = null;
    if (keys.length === 0) return;
    const p = fetchFn(keys);
    keys.forEach(k => inFlight.delete(k));
    p.then(res => {
      keys.forEach(k => {
        if (res.hasOwnProperty(k)) {
          cache.set(k, res[k]);
          const { resolve } = inFlight.get(k) || {};
          if (resolve) resolve(res[k]);
          inFlight.delete(k);
        }
      });
    }).catch(err => {
      keys.forEach(k => {
        const { reject } = inFlight.get(k) || {};
        if (reject) reject(err);
        inFlight.delete(k);
      });
    });
  }

  function enqueue(key) {
    if (cache.has(key)) return Promise.resolve(cache.get(key));
    if (inFlight.has(key)) return inFlight.get(key).promise;

    let resolve, reject;
    const promise = new Promise((res, rej) => { resolve = res; reject = rej; });
    inFlight.set(key, { promise, resolve, reject });
    batch.add(key);

    if (!timer) timer = setTimeout(flush, batchDelay);
    return promise;
  }

  return {
    get: (key) => enqueue(key),
    clearCache: () => cache.clear(),
  };
}
```

