- How would you test batching correctness with concurrent requests and simulated network delays?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests mastery of closures, promises, and per-key caching with real-world constraints like deduplicating in-flight requests, enforcing concurrency limits, and implementing TTL-based invalidation.

## Key Concepts

- **Closures and per-key cache state**: Using closures to maintain cache and in-flight maps private to each loader instance
- **In-flight deduplication**: Sharing promises for concurrent requests to the same key to prevent redundant fetches
- **Concurrency control**: Implementing a semaphore pattern with a 3-request cap
- **TTL-based caching**: Storing expiration timestamps and evicting stale entries
- **Background refresh**: Silently refetching expired keys without blocking callers
- **Invalidation semantics**: Manual cache invalidation for real-time scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
function createResourceLoader(fetcher) {
  const cache = new Map();
  const inFlight = new Map();
  let active = 0;
  const maxConcurrent = 3;
  const waiting = [];

  const acquire = () => new Promise(resolve => {
    if (active < maxConcurrent) {
      active++;
      resolve();
    } else {
      waiting.push(resolve);
    }
  });

  const release = () => {
    if (waiting.length > 0) {
      waiting.shift()();
    } else {
      active--;
    }
  };

  return {
    load(key, ttl = 5000) {
      const cached = cache.get(key);
      const now = Date.now();

      if (cached) {
        if (cached.expiry > now) {
          return cached.value;
        }
        if (!inFlight.has(key)) {
          fetcher(key).then(value => {
            cache.set(key, { value, expiry: Date.now() + ttl });
          });
        }
      }

      if (inFlight.has(key)) {
        return inFlight.get(key);
      }

      const promise = acquire().then(async () => {
        try {
          const value = await fetcher(key);
          cache.set(key, { value, expiry: Date.now() + ttl });
          return value;
        } finally {
          release();
          inFlight.delete(key);
        }
      });

      inFlight.set(key, promise);
      return promise;
    },

    invalidate(key) {
      cache.delete(key);
    }
  };
}

// Mock fetcher
const fetchUser = createResourceLoader(async (id) => {
  console.log(`Fetching user ${id}...`);
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 100));
  return { id, name: `User ${id}` };
});

// Usage
await fetchUser.load('user:1', 2000);
await fetchUser.load('user:1'); // Uses cache
fetchUser.invalidate('user:1');
await fetchUser.load('user:1'); // Refetches
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests mastery of closures, Promise coordination, and cache invalidation patterns in JavaScript without DOM dependencies.

## Key Concepts
- Closures for maintaining per-URL cache state
- Promise deduplication for concurrent in-flight requests
- Stale-while-revalidate caching strategy
- Race condition prevention and cache lifecycle management

## Code Example
```javascript
function makeFetcher() {
  const cache = new Map();
  return {
    fetchMemo: (url, ttlMs) => {
      const getEntry = () => cache.get(url) || { value: null, ts: 0, inFlight: null };
      const fetchAndUpdate = async () => {
        try {
          const response = await fetch(url);
          const data = await response.json();
          cache.set(url, { value: data, ts: Date.now(), inFlight: null });
          return data;
        } catch (error) {
          const entry = getEntry();
          entry.inFlight = null;
          throw error;
        }
      };

      return {
        get: async () => {
          const entry = getEntry();
          const now = Date.now();
          const isFresh = entry.value && (now - entry.ts) < ttlMs;

          if (isFresh) return entry.value;

          if (entry.inFlight) {
            await entry.inFlight;
            return getEntry().value;
          }

          if (entry.value) {
            entry.inFlight = fetchAndUpdate();
            return entry.value;
          }

          entry.inFlight = fetchAndUpdate();
          return await entry.inFlight;
        },
        invalidate: () => cache.delete(url)
      };
    }
  };
}
```

## Demonstration
